#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# subsequences

#% text
# `subsequences(xs)` lists every run of consecutive elements of `xs`, shortest first, the empty run included.

#% text
# `subsequences(xs, n)` gives runs of at most `n` elements, `subsequences(xs, [n])` of exactly `n`, `subsequences(xs, [a, b])` of `a` to `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Runs of one length are listed by their starting position.

#% item
# The runs keep the head of `xs`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
subsequences([a, b, c])
#% output
#= [[], [a], [b], [c], [a, b], [b, c], [a, b, c]]

#% cell
subsequences([a, b, c, d], [2])
#% output
#= [[a, b], [b, c], [c, d]]

#% cell
subsequences([a, b, c, d], 2)
#% output
#= [[], [a], [b], [c], [d], [a, b], [b, c], [c, d]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
subsequences([a, b, c, d, e], [0, 5, 2])
#% output
#= [[], [a, b], [b, c], [c, d], [d, e], [a, b, c, d], [b, c, d, e]]

#% cell
subsequences(f(a, b, c))
#% output
#= [f(), f(a), f(b), f(c), f(a, b), f(b, c), f(a, b, c)]

#% cell
length(subsequences(range(10)))
#% output
#= 56

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `subsets`, `partition`, `sequence_cases`
